Data licensing
==============

CODE in this repository is MIT licensed; see LICENSE.

DERIVED DATA produced by this repository — the conformed corpus under
data/silver/, the committed sample under data/sample/, benchmark outputs under
leaderboard/ — is released under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International
(CC BY 4.0): https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

That covers our annotations. It does not, and cannot, relicense the articles
themselves.

SOURCE ARTICLES keep their own licences, article by article.

  The committed sample under data/sample/ contains full text from the Europe PMC
  Open Access subset, and every article in it is CC BY 4.0. That is not a
  convenience: it is a filter applied at ingest. open_eviden/sources/europepmc.py
  refuses to keep an article whose licence field is anything else, because the OA
  subset also contains CC BY-NC and CC BY-NC-ND articles that we would not be
  free to redistribute here.

  Each document row carries its own `license`, `doi`, `pmcid` and `journal`, so
  the provenance of any single row is recoverable from the data rather than from
  this file. `open_eviden/qa/provenance.py` checks that every row still has them.

  If you re-run the ingester with a wider licence filter for your own use, the
  result is yours to keep and not ours to license. Do not commit it here.

  Europe PMC REST API terms: https://europepmc.org/RestfulWebService
  PubMed E-utilities terms: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK25497/

CREDENTIALED DATA is never in this repository, under any licence.

  PhysioNet datasets that require a data use agreement — MIMIC-IV among them —
  are referenced by identifier only. open_eviden/sources/physionet.py resolves an
  identifier to the landing page and the DUA you must sign; it downloads nothing
  on your behalf and writes only into data/restricted/, which git ignores.

  If you obtain such data under your own agreement, that agreement governs it.
  It does not become CC BY 4.0 by passing through this code, and derived tables
  built from it are still bound by it.

If you redistribute a corpus assembled with this tool, carry the per-article
licence and attribution through. The authors who published open access are the
reason any of this is possible.
